WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.768
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.068 --> 00:00:09.664
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:15.936
Unit six: Opinion Editorials. Today you will stress what matters with what-clauses and emphatic do.

4
00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:18.819
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:23.046
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:24.792
Op-ed.

7
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:31.196
Op-ed means an article that gives a named writer's opinion, printed opposite the editorial page.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.294
The op-ed argued for longer school days.

9
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:37.094
Your turn. Say: Op-ed.

10
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:40.749
Op-ed.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.775
Persuasive writing.

12
00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:47.803
Persuasive writing means writing meant to change what readers think or do.

13
00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:50.788
Persuasive writing needs a clear claim.

14
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.047
Your turn. Say: Persuasive writing.

15
00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.319
Persuasive writing.

16
00:00:59.083 --> 00:01:00.038
Bias.

17
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.827
Bias means an unfair leaning towards one side.

18
00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:06.521
The writer admits a personal bias.

19
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:09.502
Your turn. Say: Bias.

20
00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:13.263
Bias.

21
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:15.459
Rhetorical device.

22
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.574
Rhetorical device means a technique that makes language more persuasive.

23
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:24.019
Repetition is a powerful rhetorical device.

24
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:27.513
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical device.

25
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:31.821
Rhetorical device.

26
00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:33.654
Counterpoint.

27
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:38.136
Counterpoint means an opposing idea that is answered in the text.

28
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.249
A fair op-ed includes one counterpoint.

29
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.444
Your turn. Say: Counterpoint.

30
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.440
Counterpoint.

31
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:50.451
Call to action.

32
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.133
Call to action means a closing request that tells readers what to do.

33
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:57.914
The call to action asked readers to vote.

34
00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:01.390
Your turn. Say: Call to action.

35
00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:05.467
Call to action.

36
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:07.264
Perspective.

37
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.399
Perspective means a particular way of viewing a subject.

38
00:02:11.983 --> 00:02:14.343
The op-ed offered a farmer's perspective.

39
00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.090
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

40
00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:22.120
Perspective.

41
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:24.581
Evidence-based argument.

42
00:02:25.583 --> 00:02:29.359
Evidence-based argument means a claim supported by facts and data.

43
00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.890
An evidence-based argument persuades sceptics.

44
00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:37.331
Your turn. Say: Evidence-based argument.

45
00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:41.992
Evidence-based argument.

46
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.764
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:47.617 --> 00:02:53.508
Visal, an op-ed says 'What we need is a better debate' and 'We do need a better debate.' Why the extra words?

48
00:02:54.617 --> 00:03:05.457
They add emphasis. A what-clause puts the key idea at the end: What we need is a better debate. Emphatic do stresses the verb: We do need a better debate.

49
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.097
When should a writer use them?

50
00:03:09.183 --> 00:03:22.183
At a turning point or to answer an objection: 'Critics say the plan is costly. It does cost money, but what matters is the long-term result.' Use them sparingly.

51
00:03:23.283 --> 00:03:24.726
Any grammar rule?

52
00:03:25.817 --> 00:03:38.419
After emphatic do, use the base verb: does want, did say. In a what-clause the verb after is agrees with the focus: What matters is the evidence; What matter are the figures.

53
00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:40.986
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:46.566
Grammar focus: Emphasis: What plus clause plus is… and do plus base verb.

55
00:03:46.866 --> 00:03:47.960
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.951
What we need is a better public debate.

57
00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:53.931
What matters is the long-term result.

58
00:03:54.817 --> 00:03:57.524
The plan does cost money, but it saves more.

59
00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:07.539
A what-clause puts the key idea last: What we need is… Emphatic do / does / did + base verb stresses a verb. Use both sparingly for effect.

60
00:04:08.283 --> 00:04:12.410
Be careful! Don't say: What we need are a calm debate.

61
00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:17.074
Say: What we need is a calm debate.

62
00:04:17.374 --> 00:04:20.605
The verb must agree with the focus: a debate.

63
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.006
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.231
Writing to Persuade.

65
00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:30.492
An op-ed is persuasive writing that gives one named person's perspective.

66
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.331
What readers remember is usually the central claim, so an evidence-based argument must state it clearly.

67
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:42.492
A skilled writer does admit bias and answers one counterpoint before the end.

68
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.739
Repetition and contrast are rhetorical devices that make an argument easier to remember.

69
00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:54.299
What the final paragraph needs is a call to action that tells readers what to do next.

70
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:59.495
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.222
blank we need is more evidence.

72
00:05:02.522 --> 00:05:05.537
A, What. B, That. C, Which.

73
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.774
The answer is A: What.

74
00:05:11.917 --> 00:05:15.518
A closing request that tells readers what to do is a call to blank.

75
00:05:15.818 --> 00:05:19.240
A, bias. B, action. C, device.

76
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.927
The answer is B: action.

77
00:05:26.083 --> 00:05:28.663
Which sentence uses emphatic do correctly?

78
00:05:28.963 --> 00:05:34.138
A, She does wants change.. B, She does want change.. C, She do want change..

79
00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:40.303
The answer is B: She does want change..

80
00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:44.576
An opposing idea that is answered in the text is a blank.

81
00:05:44.876 --> 00:05:48.692
A, counterpoint. B, perspective. C, headline.

82
00:05:52.233 --> 00:05:54.477
The answer is A: counterpoint.

83
00:05:55.617 --> 00:05:59.079
According to the reading, what should the final paragraph include?

84
00:05:59.379 --> 00:06:04.495
A, a counterpoint only. B, a call to action. C, a hidden bias.

85
00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:10.404
The answer is B: a call to action.

86
00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.891
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:19.259
How is an opinion editorial different from a regular news article?

88
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:29.620
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:56.538
A news article tries to report facts neutrally, while an opinion editorial openly presents one writer's view. The op-ed uses persuasive writing, rhetorical devices and a call to action to change minds. Good op-eds still rely on an evidence-based argument and fair treatment of a counterpoint, but their purpose is to convince, not just to inform.

90
00:06:56.838 --> 00:06:58.629
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:59.517 --> 00:07:06.395
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and emphasis: what plus clause plus is… and do plus base verb.

92
00:07:06.695 --> 00:07:11.344
Today's value is critical reasoning. Test every argument before you accept it.

93
00:07:11.644 --> 00:07:13.983
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:14.283 --> 00:07:16.994
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

